From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:52:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Drive Protocol v1 negatives through the real receive()
 and generate their expectations

The negatives ran through a harness that never called receive() and could
not write a ledger, so the empty-ledger assertion was vacuous, and the
fixtures' dispositions and predicates were hand-written and contradicted the
code. receive() records every one of these refusals at both ledger levels.

Every negative now runs through the real receive() against temporary ledger
roots, with only the absent SDK's derive_execution_request and
admit_runtime_result supplied. The test asserts the real no-effect shape
(FAIL_CLOSED, received false, refusal_recorded true, NONE_REFUSAL_ONLY, no
ingress transition receipt), reads both refusal receipts back, recomputes
their digests and checks they carry the case's failure code and predicate.
Each case's expected block is written by `python3 -m
tests.test_protocol_v1_conformance_fixtures --regenerate` from a live run and
asserted equal to one.

Cases are relabelled to what they test: n3 wrong receiving operation, n4
generation-successor arithmetic, n6 absent ingress resolution. FORGED_IDENTITY,
STALE_HEAD_OR_FENCE and MISSING_AUTHORITY are not expressible on the receive()
path and are six-field spec gaps, as is payload_hash not being recomputed
from the carried manifest (PAYLOAD_HASH_NOT_RECOMPUTED_FROM_MANIFEST_AT_INGRESS).
n9 covers the non-gating case: a real Master Records propagation failure
leaves both ledger levels byte-identical and the transition replayable. n2
asserts ALLOW on the first delivery and reads the receipts back.
test-sets.json is regenerated.

Fixture evidence only; no runtime ALLOW or live receipt is claimed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
